          "court": "Court of Appeals of Maryland",
          "year": 2013,
          "date_decided": "2013-09-26",
          "holding": "The Court held that a juvenile court lacks subject-matter jurisdiction to direct how a local department, once appointed by the SSA as a foster child's representative payee, allocates the child's OASDI benefits, but required the department to notify the child's counsel when it applies to be payee and of the benefits it receives.",
          "relevance": "It is the Maryland decision on a department's use of a foster child's federal benefits to reimburse itself for the cost of his care, and on the notice that makes that practice auditable.",
          "quote": "a local department of social services need not seek permission from a juvenile court in order to exercise its statutory and regulatory-guided discretion as a duly appointed representative payee",
